Use these prompts with your project documents attached (plans, specs, addenda, RFIs, geotech, schedule, and bid form).

1. You are a senior construction estimator. Build a detailed quantity takeoff for [trade scope] from the attached drawings/specs. Output by CSI section, floor/area, and drawing detail reference.
2. Review the full bid package and create a scope checklist for [trade]. Include inclusions, exclusions, assumptions, alternates, and clarifications.
3. Compare plan set [Issue Date A] vs [Issue Date B]. List all changes that impact quantity, labor, equipment, and productivity for [trade].
4. Extract all specification requirements for [trade] and convert into a pricing checklist with measurable pay items.
5. Identify every “by others,” “NIC,” and “owner furnished” note in the documents and flag potential scope gaps.
6. Produce a labor-hour estimate for [trade scope] using crew-based logic. Show assumptions, crew mix, and production rates.
7. Build a material cost breakdown for [trade] with waste factors by material type and rationale.
8. Create an equipment estimate for [scope] including duration, utilization, mobilization, and standby risk.
9. Review civil/site drawings and quantify earthwork cut/fill, export/import, and compaction needs with risk notes.
10. Generate a drywall estimate by wall type, height band, and fire rating from plans/specs.
11. Quantify concrete scope: formwork, rebar tonnage, concrete volume, embeds, and finishing by area/element.
12. Build a structural steel estimate framework including tonnage categories, connections, deck, and erection assumptions.
13. Produce an electrical estimate checklist: feeders, branch, devices, lighting, controls, and testing/commissioning.
14. Produce a plumbing estimate checklist: domestic water, sanitary, storm, gas, fixtures, and insulation/supports.
15. Produce an HVAC estimate checklist: ductwork, piping, equipment, controls, TAB, and commissioning.
16. Analyze architectural finish schedules and generate a finish takeoff matrix by room type.
17. Convert door/hardware schedules into a pricing bill by opening type and hardware set complexity.
18. Quantify roofing scope by system type, insulation thickness, edge metal, penetrations, and warranty tier.
19. Create a masonry estimate by wall type, veneer, reinforcement, flashing, and access requirements.
20. Quantify flooring scope by material type, substrate prep requirements, transitions, and phasing constraints.
21. Review geotechnical report and identify cost impacts to foundations, slab prep, dewatering, and unsuitable soils.
22. Extract temporary works requirements (hoarding, protection, temporary utilities, shoring) and build a cost checklist.
23. Build a general conditions estimate tied to project duration, staffing, site logistics, and phasing plan.
24. Create a permit/inspection/testing allowance matrix from jurisdictional and spec requirements.
25. Identify long-lead items and propose procurement timing with escalation risk commentary.
26. Review all alternates in the bid docs and provide separate estimate buckets with scope boundaries.
27. Evaluate additive/deductive alternates and summarize probable labor and material swing by alternate.
28. Build a bid leveling template for comparing subcontractor quotes apples-to-apples.
29. Normalize these 3 subcontractor quotes and identify exclusions, hidden qualifiers, and best-value recommendation.
30. Compare vendor quotes to spec basis-of-design; flag noncompliance and equivalent-submission risk.
31. Review all addenda and produce a delta log of scope and pricing impacts.
32. Parse RFI log and identify RFIs that may change quantity, means/methods, sequencing, or responsibility.
33. Create a “known unknowns” risk register for this estimate with contingency guidance by risk category.
34. Build a contingency recommendation model: design maturity, site risk, market volatility, and schedule pressure.
35. Assess escalation exposure by commodity (steel, copper, concrete, fuel) and suggest contract protections.
36. Identify opportunities for value engineering that preserve performance while reducing installed cost.
37. Propose constructability improvements that reduce labor-hours or rework risk in [trade].
38. Review details for coordination conflicts (architectural/structural/MEP) that could cause change orders.
39. Generate a bid clarifications letter draft listing assumptions, exclusions, and unresolved scope questions.
40. Build an inclusions/exclusions table suitable for final proposal submission.
41. Validate unit price logic for [scope item] and benchmark against internal historical ranges.
42. Convert historical project data into a conceptual estimate for [new project type] with confidence bands.
43. Produce a conceptual cost per SF model by major system for early design budgeting.
44. Create a schematic-design estimate and identify missing information required for DD-level confidence.
45. Build a design-development estimate with variance analysis versus prior schematic budget.
46. Create a GMP support estimate package with assumptions log and allowance structure.
47. Generate quantity growth factors from 30% to 60% to 90% design based on this project profile.
48. Build a location factor adjustment for [city/region] and explain labor/material implications.
49. Analyze logistics constraints (urban site, laydown limits, crane access) and cost implications.
50. Price schedule acceleration options (overtime, second shift, prefabrication) and risk tradeoffs.
51. Estimate weather impact allowances by season and trade sensitivity for this schedule.
52. Create a phasing estimate for occupied renovation, including infection control/noise/night work impacts.
53. Evaluate demolition scope for hazardous materials, selective demo complexity, and disposal assumptions.
54. Extract firestopping and penetration sealing scope across all trades and quantify responsibility boundaries.
55. Build a commissioning and closeout estimate: testing, O&M docs, training, punch, and warranty callbacks.
56. Generate a subcontract buyout strategy by package size, bid coverage risk, and procurement sequence.
57. Score bid day risk by trade based on quote count, volatility, and scope clarity.
58. Create a last-minute quote comparison summary with carry numbers and recommendation.
59. Review proposal terms and identify commercial risks: pay terms, LDs, indemnity, bonds, retainage.
60. Quantify bonding/insurance impacts for this project and identify assumptions needing confirmation.
61. Build a change-order pricing template aligned with baseline estimate structure.
62. Create a labor burden calculator for [region] including taxes, benefits, small tools, and supervision.
63. Evaluate productivity assumptions against site conditions and propose adjusted production rates.
64. Generate crew loading curves by month from estimate quantities and sequence assumptions.
65. Build a cash flow curve from estimated costs and schedule milestones.
66. Produce a procurement log with bid packages, issue dates, due dates, and decision deadlines.
67. Create a quality-control cost allowance matrix tied to spec-driven mockups and testing.
68. Quantify sustainability-related scope (recycled content, EPDs, waste diversion, low-emitting materials).
69. Identify LEED or green-building documentation tasks that should be included in cost.
70. Review acoustic requirements and identify hidden cost drivers in assemblies and sealants.
71. Review envelope details and identify waterproofing continuity risks with probable cost impacts.
72. Quantify specialty equipment supports/housekeeping pads/embedded items coordination scope.
73. Produce an above-ceiling coordination risk allowance for congested MEP zones.
74. Build a prefab vs stick-build comparison for [scope], including labor, schedule, and logistics impacts.
75. Generate a target-value design estimate with cost caps by system and variance triggers.
76. Create an estimate QA/QC checklist and run it against this takeoff for error detection.
77. Audit this takeoff for duplicate quantities, missing scope, and unit inconsistencies.
78. Reconcile estimate vs bid form line items and map each scope element to the correct line.
79. Build a bid recap sheet with base bid, alternates, allowances, contingencies, and markups.
80. Separate direct costs, indirect costs, overhead, and fee with clear calculation logic.
81. Validate tax treatment by cost category and identify items commonly misclassified.
82. Build a markups waterfall (freight, waste, labor burden, supervision, OH&P) with transparent math.
83. Produce a sensitivity analysis showing cost impact of ±10% labor productivity and key material swings.
84. Run a Monte Carlo-style risk narrative (qualitative) for top 10 estimate uncertainties.
85. Create a bid/no-bid risk memo based on scope clarity, schedule, competition, and resource capacity.
86. Summarize all unanswered RFIs/submittal dependencies that create pricing uncertainty.
87. Draft bidder questions to close information gaps before bid day.
88. Generate a scope handoff package from estimating to project management with assumptions traceability.
89. Build a post-bid interview prep brief explaining major estimate drivers and qualifications.
90. Compare awarded subcontract values to estimate and explain major buyout variances.
91. Create a lessons-learned template to capture estimate misses and wins after project start.
92. Convert this estimate into a cost-loaded schedule framework for project controls.
93. Build a cost code structure aligned to estimate breakdown for field tracking.
94. Identify scope likely to become change orders due to ambiguous documents; rank by probability.
95. Produce a claims-defense documentation checklist for estimate assumptions and clarifications.
96. Create a turnkey proposal narrative from the estimate: approach, milestones, risks, and value adds.
97. Draft executive summary for client: total cost drivers, alternates, options, and recommendation.
98. Build a one-page estimate snapshot: top 15 cost items, assumptions, and decision points.
99. Translate this technical estimate into owner-friendly language without losing commercial clarity.
100. Review the full estimate package and provide a red-team critique before final submission.
